{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1793198956,"nonce":"3iUf6Ips4jn+E5DVGPIyFTXEIRc=","message":"Linker instrumentation committed as 9bd0de0a; all 40 ash_wasm_link/fiber unit tests passed. Two concurrent CLI native builds shared one cold cache and both produced the expected output 20, with zero cache errors.\n\nFirst controlled Ashui WindowPage matrix (macOS arm64, default five LLVM shards, wasm32-wasip1-threads, fibers on, its blinc_abi/xgpu/xwindow side modules staged):\n- AIR cache disabled: wall 21.868 s, LLVM IR emission 6.429 s, LLVM backend 11.333 s, linking 1.006 s. AIR lowering 0.247 s and passes 1.003 s.\n- Cold disk cache: wall 21.110 s, LLVM IR 8.827 s, backend 10.938 s, link 0.983 s; 7,104 entries written. Cache writes cost 2.231 s.\n- Warm: wall 19.643 s, 3,552/3,552 optimized hits, zero misses/writes and zero AIR lowering/pass time; cache reads cost 1.227 s. LLVM backend still 11.600 s; linking 1.012 s.\n- One source comparison changed in one callback (bytecode length unchanged, three bytes differ): 3,551 optimized hits, two misses/writes (lowered + optimized edited body), only 0.336 ms of AIR passes. Wall 21.224 s, dominated by backend variance (12.394 s).\n\nThe disabled/cold/warm WASM outputs are byte-identical: SHA256 b80dbcd62ddb24253563b8b2e91a6011f38b39dde341222a243e5a99484b5793. Warm and edited outputs also passed Ashui's real headless Chrome page smoke: 234 and 235 frames, both exited 0. Peak process RSS was 1.93-2.02 GB; disk entry payloads total about 5.24 MB.\n\nThe linker is not the dominant stage here: warm input read/parse 35.947 ms, resolve 48.945 ms, layout/reachability 280.965 ms, relocations 65.955 ms, emission 39.691 ms, fiber transform 521.380 ms, whole link 1,012.073 ms. LLVM backend is 11-12 s. This application produces 8,922 LLVM bodies and all AOT bytecode bodies are compiled before later sweeps; function/trampoline registries retain addresses.\n\nAIR reuse is working, but on this app about one second of AIR passes is replaced by about one second of cache reads, so an overall speedup should not be inferred from a single wall-time sample. Found an additional source of repeated frontend work: each LLVM function creates fresh lookup maps and frameless caches. A follow-up shares that state within the build, keeping lowered-body residency bounded at 2 MiB and optimized residency at 8 MiB. Seven cache unit tests now pass, including transitive dependency invalidation after a memoized frameless refusal and release of the shared workspace at build exit; release process tests and a second timing matrix are running.","files":null}]}